# 7. Firestore Database Architecture

Design a normalized, scalable Firestore document structure.

Firestore is a document database, not a SQL database. Do not create SQL tables or attempt to execute SQL queries.

Use collections and documents with explicit relationships.

The following structure is the required baseline. You may refine it where the existing codebase requires, but preserve the logical relationships and security boundaries.

## 7.1 users collection

Path:

users/{uid}

Fields:

- uid
- employeeId
- name
- email
- role: ADMIN or EMPLOYEE
- department
- designation
- accountStatus: ACTIVE or DEACTIVATED
- createdAt
- updatedAt
- createdBy
- lastLoginAt (if maintained securely)

Rules:

- Firebase UID is the primary identity.
- Employee ID must be unique.
- Employee ID must not be used as a substitute for Firebase UID.
- Passwords must never be stored here.
- Employees cannot change their own role.
- Employees cannot activate themselves after deactivation.

## 7.2 tasks collection

Path:

tasks/{taskId}

Fields:

- taskId
- title
- description
- priority
- category
- department
- assignmentType: SOLO or GROUP
- assignedBy
- assignedEmployeeIds
- createdAt
- updatedAt
- deadline
- status
- taskNote
- reminderSettings
- reviewStatus
- cancelledAt
- cancelledBy

Use Firestore Timestamp values for dates.

Do not store critical dates as inconsistent formatted strings.

The interface can display dates in DD/MM/YYYY format.

Use Asia/Kolkata as the application's business timezone.

A group task must be represented as ONE shared task document.

Do not duplicate a group task for every employee.

## 7.3 taskMembers collection

Path:

tasks/{taskId}/members/{uid}

Fields:

- uid
- employeeId
- employeeName
- assignedAt
- individualStatus
- individualSubmissionStatus
- submissionText or submissionNote, if the existing interface supports it
- submittedAt
- lastStatusUpdatedAt
- blockerDescription
- blockerReportedAt
- approvedAt
- approvedBy
- revisionRequestedAt
- revisionReason
- updatedAt

This collection is extremely important.

A shared task must maintain separate progress for every assigned employee.

For example:

Task: Prepare quarterly report.

Assigned employees:
- Employee A
- Employee B
- Employee C

All three must reference the same taskId.

Each employee must have their own taskMembers document.

If Employee A submits the task, only Employee A's submission state changes.

Employee B and Employee C must remain independently pending or in progress.

A group task becomes ready for group-level review only when every required member has submitted, unless the administrator explicitly reviews individual submissions earlier.

Do not let one member's submission automatically complete the entire group task.

## 7.4 statusRequests collection

Path:

statusRequests/{requestId}

Fields:

- requestId
- taskId
- employeeId
- createdBy
- question
- requestType
- createdAt
- response
- respondedAt
- status: PENDING, RESPONDED, CLOSED

Each employee's response must be independently stored.

For group tasks, the manager must be able to request updates from all members or selected members.

Do not assume one response represents the entire group.

## 7.5 queries collection

Path:

queries/{queryId}

Fields:

- queryId
- taskId
- employeeId
- employeeName
- title
- description
- category
- priority
- status: OPEN, ANSWERED, RESOLVED
- createdAt
- updatedAt
- resolvedAt
- resolvedBy

## 7.6 queryMessages subcollection

Path:

queries/{queryId}/messages/{messageId}

Fields:

- messageId
- senderUid
- senderRole
- message
- createdAt

Preserve the complete query conversation.

Resolving a query must not automatically complete its associated task.

## 7.7 taskMessages subcollection

Path:

tasks/{taskId}/messages/{messageId}

Fields:

- messageId
- senderUid
- senderName
- senderRole
- message
- createdAt

This is for task-specific group communication.

Only authorized task participants and administrators may access the conversation.

Do not expose one task's conversation to unrelated employees.

## 7.8 notifications collection

Path:

notifications/{notificationId}

Fields:

- notificationId
- recipientUid
- type
- title
- message
- relatedTaskId
- relatedEntityId
- createdAt
- read
- readAt
- navigationTarget

Notifications must be stored persistently.

Each recipient must have their own notification record.

Do not create one shared notification document that employees can accidentally modify for each other.

## 7.9 reminderSettings collection

Path:

reminderSettings/{uid}

Fields:

- uid
- enabled
- reminderTimes
- timezone: Asia/Kolkata
- updatedAt
- updatedBy

Store reminder preferences and configuration.

## 7.10 reminderOccurrences collection

