# 9. Firebase Security Rules

Create proper Firestore security rules.

Since privileged writes and business operations are handled through Express using Firebase Admin SDK, do not create broad client-side write permissions.

Important:

Firebase Admin SDK bypasses Firestore Security Rules.

Therefore, your Express backend must independently enforce all authorization and validation rules.

The Firestore rules should deny unauthenticated access and restrict direct client access to the minimum required.

If the application reads data exclusively through Express, deny direct client reads and writes to application collections.

If you implement direct client-side realtime listeners for selected data, allow only strictly scoped reads for the authenticated owner or authorized task participant.

Never permit arbitrary employee queries across the entire users or tasks collection.

Never permit employees to modify role, account status, task ownership, review decisions, or audit events.

Provide separate rules for every collection and subcollection.

Create and document all required Firestore indexes.

Do not use permissive development rules such as:

allow read, write: if true;

Do not deploy insecure temporary rules as a permanent solution.

